msgid "terminology.term.bit.title"
msgstr "Bit"

msgid "terminology.term.bit.body"
msgstr "The smallest unit of information used here, representing binary 0 or 1. Several bits combine into a larger value; an 8-bit value contains eight bits."

msgid "terminology.term.binary.title"
msgstr "Binary"

msgid "terminology.term.binary.body"
msgstr "A way to represent information using only 0 and 1. Digital circuits map those values to low and high signal levels."

msgid "terminology.term.signal.title"
msgstr "Signal"

msgid "terminology.term.signal.body"
msgstr "A logic state carried between ports and wires. This game mainly uses low 0, high 1, and high-impedance Z."

msgid "terminology.term.low_level.title"
msgstr "Low level (0 / Low)"

msgid "terminology.term.low_level.body"
msgstr "The signal state representing logic 0. The Test Bench uses a red circular marker for a low input."

msgid "terminology.term.high_level.title"
msgstr "High level (1 / High)"

msgid "terminology.term.high_level.body"
msgstr "The signal state representing logic 1. The Test Bench uses a green diamond marker for a high input."

msgid "terminology.term.high_impedance.title"
msgstr "High impedance (High-Z / Z)"

msgid "terminology.term.high_impedance.body"
msgstr "A port state that drives neither 0 nor 1, as though disconnected from the line. It is not a third Boolean value; the UI shows it in gray."

msgid "terminology.term.short_circuit.title"
msgstr "Signal conflict (Short Circuit)"

msgid "terminology.term.short_circuit.body"
msgstr "A single input is driven low and high at the same time. The simulator reports this as a short circuit or driver conflict so the wiring can be corrected."

msgid "terminology.term.logic_gate.title"
msgstr "Logic Gate"

msgid "terminology.term.logic_gate.body"
msgstr "A basic combinational component that calculates output signals from input signals. NOT, AND, OR, XOR, and NOR are logic gates."

msgid "terminology.term.not_gate.title"
msgstr "NOT gate"

msgid "terminology.term.not_gate.body"
msgstr "Inverts its input: 0 becomes 1, and 1 becomes 0."

msgid "terminology.term.and_gate.title"
msgstr "AND gate"

msgid "terminology.term.and_gate.body"
msgstr "Outputs 1 only when all inputs are 1; otherwise it outputs 0."

msgid "terminology.term.or_gate.title"
msgstr "OR gate"

msgid "terminology.term.or_gate.body"
msgstr "Outputs 1 when at least one input is 1; it outputs 0 only when every input is 0."

msgid "terminology.term.xor_gate.title"
msgstr "XOR gate"

msgid "terminology.term.xor_gate.body"
msgstr "Outputs 1 when its two inputs differ and 0 when they match. A half adder commonly uses it to produce SUM."

msgid "terminology.term.nor_gate.title"
msgstr "NOR gate"

msgid "terminology.term.nor_gate.body"
msgstr "Performs OR and then inverts the result. It outputs 1 only when every input is 0; cross-coupled NOR gates can form an SR latch."

msgid "terminology.term.input_output.title"
msgstr "Input / Output"

msgid "terminology.term.input_output.body"
msgstr "An input receives a signal and an output produces one. Connections must run from an output port to an input port."

msgid "terminology.term.port.title"
msgstr "Port"

msgid "terminology.term.port.body"
msgstr "A connection point on the edge of a component that receives or sends a signal. Ports have a direction and may be named A, B, SUM, and so on."

msgid "terminology.term.wire.title"
msgstr "Wire"

msgid "terminology.term.wire.body"
msgstr "A line that connects two ports and carries a signal. Ordinary wires describe connectivity and add no modeled latency in this game."

msgid "terminology.term.junction.title"
msgstr "Junction / Branch"

msgid "terminology.term.junction.body"
msgstr "A node that distributes one output signal to several destinations. A branch shares the same signal rather than duplicating a calculation."

msgid "terminology.term.combinational_loop.title"
msgstr "Combinational Loop"

msgid "terminology.term.combinational_loop.body"
msgstr "A path where combinational output returns to its own input without a storage element in between. It may never settle, so the simulator rejects it."

msgid "terminology.term.tick.title"
msgstr "tick / propagation step"

msgid "terminology.term.tick.body"
msgstr "One step of signal propagation through a circuit. Gates settle after one or more ticks; a tick is not the same as a CPU cycle in the system chapters."

msgid "terminology.term.truth_table.title"
msgstr "Truth Table"

msgid "terminology.term.truth_table.body"
msgstr "A table listing every input combination and the expected outputs. Two inputs A and B produce the four rows 00, 01, 10, and 11."

msgid "terminology.term.topology.title"
msgstr "Topology"

msgid "terminology.term.topology.body"
msgstr "The structure formed by components and their connections. Topology describes what is connected, not where a component happens to appear on screen."

msgid "terminology.term.test_bench.title"
msgstr "Test Bench"

msgid "terminology.term.test_bench.body"
msgstr "A tool that supplies inputs, runs tests, and checks circuit or system outputs. It exposes behavior without constructing the answer for the player."

msgid "terminology.term.debug_run.title"
msgstr "Debug Run"

msgid "terminology.term.debug_run.body"
msgstr "A check using only the currently selected input or case, useful for locating a problem. Passing a debug run does not mean the whole level passes."

msgid "terminology.term.official_test.title"
msgstr "Official Test Set"

msgid "terminology.term.official_test.body"
msgstr "The complete set of cases required by a level. A level passes only when every official case satisfies its correctness and target conditions."

msgid "terminology.term.trace.title"
msgstr "Trace (execution trace)"

msgid "terminology.term.trace.body"
msgstr "An ordered sequence of event evidence already determined by the simulator. Playback, pause, stepping, and the adjustable Clock Period change only how it is viewed."

msgid "terminology.term.clock_period.title"
msgstr "Clock Period"

msgid "terminology.term.clock_period.body"
msgstr "How long one visible trace step remains on screen. A smaller value plays faster; it changes observation pace only, never simulation ticks, results, metrics, or official-test verdicts."

msgid "terminology.term.abstraction.title"
msgstr "Abstraction"

msgid "terminology.term.abstraction.body"
msgstr "Hiding internal detail while retaining useful external behavior and ports. Abstraction lets small components become building blocks for larger systems."

msgid "terminology.term.encapsulation.title"
msgstr "Encapsulation"

msgid "terminology.term.encapsulation.body"
msgstr "Saving a verified circuit as a reusable component. Its source circuit is retained, while later levels can work through the simpler external interface."

msgid "terminology.term.half_adder.title"
msgstr "Half Adder (HalfAdder)"

msgid "terminology.term.half_adder.body"
msgstr "A circuit that adds two one-bit inputs A and B and produces SUM and CARRY. It has no carry input from a lower bit."

msgid "terminology.term.sum.title"
msgstr "SUM"

msgid "terminology.term.sum.body"
msgstr "The result at the current bit position of an adder. In a half adder, SUM is 1 when A and B differ."

msgid "terminology.term.carry.title"
msgstr "CARRY"

msgid "terminology.term.carry.body"
msgstr "A value passed to the next higher bit when addition overflows the current bit. In a half adder, CARRY is 1 only when A and B are both 1."

msgid "terminology.term.full_adder.title"
msgstr "Full Adder"

msgid "terminology.term.full_adder.body"
msgstr "Adds the one-bit values A, B, and carry input CIN, producing SUM and COUT. Several full adders can be chained into a multi-bit adder."

msgid "terminology.term.cin_cout.title"
msgstr "CIN / COUT (carry in / carry out)"

msgid "terminology.term.cin_cout.body"
msgstr "CIN receives carry from a lower bit and COUT sends carry to a higher bit. The lowest bit normally uses CIN=0."

msgid "terminology.term.multiplexer.title"
msgstr "Multiplexer (MUX)"

msgid "terminology.term.multiplexer.body"
msgstr "Selects one of several inputs and forwards it to the output according to control signals. SOURCE_MUX and RESULT_MUX are data selectors."

msgid "terminology.term.alu.title"
msgstr "ALU (Arithmetic Logic Unit)"

msgid "terminology.term.alu.body"
msgstr "Combinational hardware that performs addition, logic, and related operations. ALU4 means an ALU that processes four bits at once."

msgid "terminology.term.opcode.title"
msgstr "Opcode / OP"

msgid "terminology.term.opcode.body"
msgstr "A control value that tells an ALU or controller which operation to perform. OP0 and OP1 are control bits that form an opcode."

msgid "terminology.term.latch.title"
msgstr "Latch"

msgid "terminology.term.latch.body"
msgstr "A storage circuit that can retain one bit. Unlike pure combinational logic, its current output can depend on earlier input."

msgid "terminology.term.sr_latch.title"
msgstr "SR Latch (SRLatch)"

msgid "terminology.term.sr_latch.body"
msgstr "A one-bit latch controlled by SET and RESET, commonly built from two cross-coupled NOR gates."

msgid "terminology.term.set_reset.title"
msgstr "SET / RESET"

msgid "terminology.term.set_reset.body"
msgstr "SET requests a stored 1 and RESET requests a stored 0. An SR latch should not activate both at the same time."

msgid "terminology.term.d_q.title"
msgstr "D / Q / NQ"

msgid "terminology.term.d_q.body"
msgstr "D is the data input to be stored, Q is the current stored output, and NQ is the inverse of Q."

msgid "terminology.term.register.title"
msgstr "Register"

msgid "terminology.term.register.body"
msgstr "A small, fast storage component made from several one-bit cells. Register4 stores four bits; LOAD=1 writes D and LOAD=0 holds the current value."

msgid "terminology.term.decoder.title"
msgstr "Decoder"

msgid "terminology.term.decoder.body"
msgstr "Converts address or control bits into selection signals so that one target among several is activated."

msgid "terminology.term.address_write.title"
msgstr "ADDR / WRITE"

msgid "terminology.term.address_write.body"
msgstr "ADDR selects a storage location. WRITE determines whether the access stores a new value or only reads the existing value."

msgid "terminology.term.ram.title"
msgstr "RAM (Random Access Memory)"

msgid "terminology.term.ram.body"
msgstr "Storage hardware that reads and writes data by address. A 2×4 RAM has two four-bit locations; RAM64x8 has sixty-four eight-bit locations."

msgid "terminology.term.cpu.title"
msgstr "CPU (Central Processing Unit)"

msgid "terminology.term.cpu.body"
msgstr "A processor that reads instructions, performs calculations, and exchanges data with RAM through a Bus. CPU8 names the eight-bit processor source in this game."

msgid "terminology.term.bus.title"
msgstr "Bus"

msgid "terminology.term.bus.body"
msgstr "A shared channel carrying requests, addresses, and data between components such as CPU and RAM. Bus2 and Bus8 indicate the number of bits transferred per stage."

msgid "terminology.term.accumulator.title"
msgstr "Accumulator (ACC)"

msgid "terminology.term.accumulator.body"
msgstr "A CPU register holding the current arithmetic result. Many instructions read ACC, calculate through the ALU, and write the result back to ACC."

msgid "terminology.term.controller.title"
msgstr "Controller / CONTROL"

msgid "terminology.term.controller.body"
msgstr "Produces selection, load, and write signals from the current instruction, arranging what the data path does at each step."

msgid "terminology.term.data_path.title"
msgstr "Data Path"

msgid "terminology.term.data_path.body"
msgstr "The routes along which data moves and is calculated through registers, MUXes, the ALU, Bus, and RAM. TinyComputer connects these parts into an executable machine."

msgid "terminology.term.load.title"
msgstr "LOAD"

msgid "terminology.term.load.body"
msgstr "Moves an immediate or memory value into a CPU register. LOAD_IMM uses a constant in the instruction; LOAD_MEM reads from RAM."

msgid "terminology.term.store.title"
msgstr "STORE"

msgid "terminology.term.store.body"
msgstr "Writes a value from a CPU register into RAM. STORE_MEM and STORE [address] both describe a memory write."

msgid "terminology.term.immediate.title"
msgstr "Immediate (IMM)"

msgid "terminology.term.immediate.body"
msgstr "A constant written directly in an instruction instead of fetched from RAM first. ADD_IMM and LOAD_IMM both use immediate values."

msgid "terminology.term.wraparound.title"
msgstr "Wraparound"

msgid "terminology.term.wraparound.body"
msgstr "Continuing from the other end of a fixed-width range after overflow. An 8-bit addition wraps modulo 256 and keeps only the lowest eight bits."

msgid "terminology.term.program.title"
msgstr "Program"

msgid "terminology.term.program.body"
msgstr "Instructions or code describing the operations a machine performs in order. The Program panel lets the player edit, explain, and apply a program."

msgid "terminology.term.dsl.title"
msgstr "DSL (Domain-Specific Language)"

msgid "terminology.term.dsl.body"
msgstr "A small language designed for a particular task. This game uses a Python-like DSL for loops, load, store, and compute operations, but it is not full Python."

msgid "terminology.term.apply.title"
msgstr "Apply Program"

msgid "terminology.term.apply.body"
msgstr "Parses and confirms the current editor contents so the next run uses them. An unapplied draft does not silently change the applied program."

msgid "terminology.term.workload.title"
msgstr "Workload"

msgid "terminology.term.workload.body"
msgstr "The combination of data, instructions, and repetitions a machine must process in an experiment. Performance comparisons should use the same workload unless it is the controlled change."

msgid "terminology.term.cycle.title"
msgstr "cycle (system cycle)"

msgid "terminology.term.cycle.body"
msgstr "A unit of time in the system performance model. Total cycles combine compute, wait, memory service, and bus transfer events."

msgid "terminology.term.latency.title"
msgstr "Latency"

msgid "terminology.term.latency.body"
msgstr "The time from starting one operation until its result becomes available. Greater RAM service latency usually makes each read take longer."

msgid "terminology.term.bandwidth.title"
msgstr "Bandwidth"

msgid "terminology.term.bandwidth.body"
msgstr "The amount of data a channel can transfer per unit of time. A wider Bus reduces the number of transfer stages needed for the same byte."

msgid "terminology.term.throughput.title"
msgstr "Throughput"

msgid "terminology.term.throughput.body"
msgstr "The rate at which a system completes work over time. It is related to the latency of individual operations, but the two are not the same metric."

msgid "terminology.term.serialization.title"
msgstr "Serialization"

msgid "terminology.term.serialization.body"
msgstr "Splitting data into consecutive transfer stages when a channel cannot carry the whole value at once. A narrow Bus increases stages per byte."

msgid "terminology.term.cpu_wait.title"
msgstr "CPU WAIT"

msgid "terminology.term.cpu_wait.body"
msgstr "Cycles where the CPU has issued a request but cannot compute until the Bus or RAM returns data. It is key evidence when diagnosing the von Neumann bottleneck."

msgid "terminology.term.profiler.title"
msgstr "Profiler"

msgid "terminology.term.profiler.body"
msgstr "A tool that summarizes a completed Trace into total cycles, CPU WAIT, RAM service, Bus transfer, and related metrics. It reads evidence without changing the simulation."

msgid "terminology.term.prediction.title"
msgstr "Prediction"

msgid "terminology.term.prediction.body"
msgstr "A testable expectation about an experiment's result and cause, made before running it. Locking a prediction prevents an explanation from being invented after seeing the answer."

msgid "terminology.term.baseline.title"
msgstr "Baseline"

msgid "terminology.term.baseline.body"
msgstr "A reference run recorded before changing a factor. Only a baseline under matching conditions gives the later difference a clear meaning."

msgid "terminology.term.controlled_change.title"
msgstr "Controlled Change"

msgid "terminology.term.controlled_change.body"
msgstr "Changing one factor under investigation while keeping the others fixed. This makes a Before → After difference attributable to that factor."

msgid "terminology.term.bottleneck.title"
msgstr "Bottleneck"

msgid "terminology.term.bottleneck.body"
msgstr "The main part limiting further speedup of a whole system. Improving a non-bottleneck may have little effect, so use Trace and metrics to locate it."

msgid "terminology.term.hardware_cost.title"
msgstr "Hardware Cost"

msgid "terminology.term.hardware_cost.body"
msgstr "The game's simplified cost score for faster or larger hardware. A cycle target should still be judged against how much hardware was used to reach it."

msgid "terminology.term.before_after.title"
msgstr "Before → After"

msgid "terminology.term.before_after.body"
msgstr "A side-by-side comparison of evidence before and after a controlled change, including cycles, wait, data movement, and cost rather than correctness alone."

msgid "terminology.term.deterministic.title"
msgstr "Deterministic"

msgid "terminology.term.deterministic.body"
msgstr "The same program, machine, and input always produce the same Trace and metrics. The adjustable Clock Period and interface animation cannot change the simulation result."

msgid "terminology.term.cache.title"
msgstr "Cache"

msgid "terminology.term.cache.body"
msgstr "Small, fast hardware storage near the CPU that retains blocks recently fetched from RAM, reducing repeated long-distance reads."

msgid "terminology.term.cache_line.title"
msgstr "Cache Line"

msgid "terminology.term.cache_line.body"
msgstr "The block of data fetched and stored as one unit between Cache and RAM. Nearby accesses within the same line can then hit."

msgid "terminology.term.hit.title"
msgstr "Hit (Cache Hit)"

msgid "terminology.term.hit.body"
msgstr "The requested data's line is already in Cache, so it can return from nearby without another RAM access."

msgid "terminology.term.miss.title"
msgstr "Miss (Cache Miss)"

msgid "terminology.term.miss.body"
msgstr "The requested data's line is absent from Cache, so the system must fetch it from RAM and the CPU usually waits."

msgid "terminology.term.evict.title"
msgstr "Evict / Replacement"

msgid "terminology.term.evict.body"
msgstr "Removing an existing line when Cache needs room for a new one. Evicting a line needed again soon can cause another Miss."

msgid "terminology.term.fill.title"
msgstr "Cache Fill"

msgid "terminology.term.fill.body"
msgstr "Placing the full line returned from RAM into Cache after a Miss, allowing later requests to that line to hit."

msgid "terminology.term.locality.title"
msgstr "Locality"

msgid "terminology.term.locality.body"
msgstr "A program's tendency to reuse data close in time or address. Good locality lets even a small Cache eliminate many distant accesses."

msgid "terminology.term.spatial_locality.title"
msgstr "Spatial Locality"

msgid "terminology.term.spatial_locality.body"
msgstr "The tendency to access nearby addresses soon after one address. Scanning consecutive values in one cache line uses spatial locality."

msgid "terminology.term.temporal_locality.title"
msgstr "Temporal Locality"

msgid "terminology.term.temporal_locality.body"
msgstr "The tendency to access the same data again soon. The earlier reuse happens, the more likely the data is still in Cache."

msgid "terminology.term.access_order.title"
msgstr "Access Order"

msgid "terminology.term.access_order.body"
msgstr "The sequence in which a program reads or writes addresses. Two orders with the same final result can have very different Cache behavior."

msgid "terminology.term.row_first.title"
msgstr "row-first"

msgid "terminology.term.row_first.body"
msgstr "Finishing consecutive elements in one row before moving to the next. For this game's row-major matrix, it usually reuses each cache line continuously."

msgid "terminology.term.column_first.title"
msgstr "column-first"

msgid "terminology.term.column_first.body"
msgstr "Stepping across rows down one column before moving to the next. In row-major storage, this can rotate through lines and cause repeated Misses."

msgid "terminology.term.working_set.title"
msgstr "Working Set"

msgid "terminology.term.working_set.body"
msgstr "All data used frequently during one phase of a program. If it is larger than Cache capacity, even a good access order may cause repeated replacement."

msgid "terminology.term.pass.title"
msgstr "Pass"

msgid "terminology.term.pass.body"
msgstr "One complete round of processing over a data set. Two passes may perform different calculations while needing the same data again."

msgid "terminology.term.work_group.title"
msgstr "Work Group"

msgid "terminology.term.work_group.body"
msgstr "A smaller data group carved from a larger workload so several passes can finish it before moving to the next group."

msgid "terminology.term.blocking.title"
msgstr "Blocking"

msgid "terminology.term.blocking.body"
msgstr "Reorganizing computation around small data blocks so the active working set fits more easily in Cache. It changes order without changing the intended result."

msgid "terminology.term.tiling.title"
msgstr "Tiling"

msgid "terminology.term.tiling.body"
msgstr "A common name for Blocking that emphasizes cutting a matrix or data space into tiles and completing one tile at a time."

msgid "terminology.term.data_request.title"
msgstr "Data Request"

msgid "terminology.term.data_request.body"
msgstr "A system operation started by the CPU to read or write an address. It may hit in Cache or travel through the Bus to RAM."

msgid "terminology.example.title"
msgstr "A concrete example"

msgid "terminology.term.multiplexer.example"
msgstr "Suppose the four inputs are D0=3, D1=7, D2=12, and D3=1. With OP=10 (binary 2), the MUX forwards only D2, so the output is 12. The other values are present but do not pass through. Think of it as a four-way junction controlled by OP."

msgid "terminology.term.alu.example"
msgstr "Let A=6 and B=3. With OP set to ADD, the result is 9. With OP set to AND, binary 0110 AND 0011 produces 0010, or 2. The inputs stay the same; changing OP tells the ALU to perform a different operation."

msgid "terminology.term.sr_latch.example"
msgstr "First apply S=1 and R=0 to set Q to 1. Return both inputs to 0 and Q remains 1—that is the stored memory. Apply S=0 and R=1 to clear Q to 0. On a NOR SR latch, do not make S and R both 1."

msgid "terminology.term.decoder.example"
msgstr "A two-bit address can be 00, 01, 10, or 11. With ADDR=01, only W1 is 1 and the other three selection lines are 0. Connect those lines to four registers' LOAD inputs, and only register 1 accepts new data."

msgid "terminology.term.bus.example"
msgstr "To transfer the eight-bit value 10100101, Bus8 can carry it in one stage. Bus2 can carry only two bits at a time, so it sends 10, 10, 01, and 01 across four stages. A narrower channel usually remains occupied longer by the same value."

msgid "terminology.term.accumulator.example"
msgstr "Suppose ACC starts at 3. For ADD_IMM 2, the controller lets the ALU read ACC=3 and the immediate value 2, calculate 5, and write 5 back to ACC. The next ADD_IMM 4 now reads ACC=5 and finishes at 9. ACC carries one result into the next calculation."

msgid "terminology.term.serialization.example"
msgstr "The eight-bit value 10100101 must be split into four consecutive pieces on Bus2. The receiver cannot use the complete byte until every piece arrives. Those four pieces taking turns on the channel are the extra delay caused by serialization."

msgid "terminology.term.cpu_wait.example"
msgstr "At cycle 0, the CPU requests a value from RAM. RAM and the Bus spend the next 12 cycles servicing and returning it. Without the operand, the CPU shows WAIT. Only when the data arrives at cycle 13 can the next calculation begin."

msgid "terminology.term.bottleneck.example"
msgstr "Suppose one job spends 2 cycles in the CPU, 4 on the Bus, and 12 in RAM. Speeding the CPU from 2 to 1 saves only one cycle, while reducing RAM from 12 to 4 saves eight. RAM is the main bottleneck in this case."

msgid "terminology.term.cache.example"
msgstr "The first read of address 2 is not in Cache, so it Misses and fetches the whole cache line containing addresses 0–3 from RAM. A following read of address 3 finds its data in that line and Hits, avoiding another RAM access."

msgid "terminology.term.working_set.example"
msgstr "A phase repeatedly uses lines 0, 1, 2, and 3, so those four lines form its working set. If Cache has one slot, every new line evicts the previous one. On the second pass, all four accesses may Miss again."

msgid "terminology.term.blocking.example"
msgstr "The original order performs pass 1 on lines 0 and 1, then returns for pass 2; a tiny Cache may have evicted line 0 by then. Blocking finishes both passes on line 0 before moving to line 1, making the second pass more likely to Hit. The work is unchanged; only its order moves."

